    width = { this.axis == "x" ? this.span() : this.thickness },
    height = { this.axis == "x" ? this.thickness : this.span() },

    // the rule itself, so `inset` can move its leading edge without the Divider
    // authoring an `x` a parent layout may be arranging

    rule: View [
        x = { classroot.axis == "x" ? classroot.inset : 0 },
        y = { classroot.axis == "x" ? 0 : classroot.inset },
        width = { classroot.axis == "x" ? Math.max(0, parent.width - classroot.inset) : classroot.thickness },
        height = { classroot.axis == "x" ? classroot.thickness : Math.max(0, parent.height - classroot.inset) },
        fill = { classroot.color } ]
    ]

/*
# ResponsiveLayout — arrange children differently at different widths.

A view's children often want one arrangement when there is room and another
when there isn't: three panels side by side on a wide screen, the same panels
stacked on a phone; an optional control that appears only when space allows.
ResponsiveLayout states those alternatives directly. You give it a list of
**plans** — each one says *"from this width up, arrange the children like
this"* — and it keeps the right plan applied as the view's width changes.
Hiding, showing, and dividing the width are all part of a plan, not separate
wiring.

    bar: View [
        layout: ResponsiveLayout [ plan = { [
            { from: 900, flow: "row",  share: { logo: 15, nav: 55, actions: 30 } },
            { from: 600, flow: "row",  share: { logo: 20, nav: 80, actions: 0 } },
            { from: 0,   flow: "stack" },
        ] } ],
        logo:    Logo    [ ],
        nav:     Nav     [ ],
        actions: Actions [ ],
    ]

Each entry's `from` means *use this plan when the view is at least this wide*
— the entry with the largest `from` that fits wins, wherever it sits in the
list, and an entry with `from: 0` is the fallback for anything narrower.
`flow` is the arrangement: `"row"` places children left to right; `"stack"`
places them top to bottom. **`share` divides width in either flow** — in a
row it apportions the run; in a stack it sets how wide each band is, as a
plain percentage of the view. The names are the view's own child names,
checked when the layout first arranges (a name with no child is a loud
error). `logo: 15` gives logo 15% of it; **a share of `0` drops that child
while the plan is active** — so "the actions disappear on small screens" is
just a number in the narrow plan, and they reappear by themselves when the
view widens. `gap` sets the space between children (an entry may carry its
own `gap` to override the layout's). Heights are never touched: children keep
their own heights, and the view's height falls out of its content, as always.

So the example reads as a storyboard: at 900 and up, a three-across row split
15/55/30; between 600 and 900, the actions drop and the nav takes their room;
below 600, everything stacks.

**A plan need not name every child, and 20% means 20% of what is left.** A
child the plan leaves out keeps its natural width and the flow just places
it — and the run a row's shares divide is what remains **after the gaps and
after those naturals**. So a fixed-width logo beside two proportional regions
composes without the author computing a percentage for the logo:

    layout: ResponsiveLayout [ gap = 10, plan = { [
        { from: 0, flow: "row", share: { nav: 60, actions: 40 } },
    ] } ],
    logo:    View [ width = 200 ],          // not named: keeps its 200
    nav:     Nav  [ ],                      // 60% of what is left
    actions: Actions [ ],                   // 40% of what is left

In an 880-wide bar that is 200 for the logo and 396 / 264 for the two
regions — the row fills, exactly. Change the logo's width and the regions
answer; they were never told about it.

**`share: { logo: "auto" }` says that out loud.** The word means precisely
what leaving the child out means — keep its natural size, divide the rest —
so it buys no new geometry, only legibility: the plan reads as a complete
statement of the row, and a name in a plan is checked against the tree, where
an omission is mentioned nowhere. It is **not** `share: 0`, which is the
opposite: `0` drops the child and reclaims its space, `"auto"` keeps the
child at the size it chose for itself. In a stack `"auto"` is accepted and
changes nothing — the flow axis there is height, which a share never touches.

**Two shares are refused, where the plan is read** — both of them mistakes
with no arrangement they could mean, and both silent before. A row's shares
divide one run, so a row whose shares add to more than 100 is an error naming
the plan and the total, rather than a row that runs its children off the end.
And a share that is neither a number from 0 to 100 nor the word `"auto"` is
an error naming the child, rather than a `NaN` width on it and a `NaN`
position on its neighbours.

**The two things authors miss.** `share` is not row-only: a `"stack"` entry
may carry shares too, and they set each band's width — the narrow plan above
could say `{ from: 0, flow: "stack", share: { logo: 100, nav: 100 } }` to make
both bands full-bleed. (A stack's shares are independent percentages of the
view, not slices of one run, which is why two hundreds are fine there and a
row's total is held to 100.) And `share: 0` is the whole of "hide it when
it's tight": a child dropped this way leaves the flow entirely and its space
is reclaimed, so there is no reason to branch a tree on a width
(`narrow ? … : …`) — appearing and disappearing is one number in one plan,
and the plan is the only place the breakpoint is written down.

**Slack and alignment.** When a row's children keep natural widths, the run
may not fill the view — and where the run sits is said with structure, not a
knob: a `Spacer [ ]` child absorbs the slack. Between two groups it pushes
them apart; one on each side centers the run; one first right-aligns it. On
the axis the flow does not manage, a child places itself with the ordinary
literals (`y = center` in a row). Shares are cut from what the naturals
leave, so shares adding to 100 leave no slack at all and a `Spacer` beside
them collapses — *shares fill; naturals align.*

**Nesting — who decides what.** A ResponsiveLayout watches its own view's
width — not the window's. The parent's plan decides how much room each child
gets; each child decides what to do with the room it got — if the child has
its own ResponsiveLayout, its plans answer to its own width, automatically.
Room flows down; each level responds locally. (A plan can only name direct
children — managing a grandchild is the child's own plan's business.)

**Division of labor.** ResponsiveLayout manages **named regions**. A collection
of uniform, replicated cards that should reflow to fit is the other tool:
`WrappingLayout` (it wraps by fitting, no plan needed) with a width
constraint on the card.

**Padding, and what `from` then means.** The inset is the view's
(`View.padding`), and a plan is selected against the **content** width, with
the shares dividing it — so a padded bar's tiers answer to the room its
children actually get, and `from: 600` means 600 of usable room, not 600 of
outer width.

**When the rules aren't enough.** A plan speaks width, flow, share, and gap.
Whole alternate configurations beyond geometry are a `State` applied on a
width condition; fully custom geometry is a subclass overriding `place()` —
the same seam every layout in the library is built on.

## plan
The storyboard: a list of `{ from, flow, share?, gap? }` entries. **`from` means "use
this plan when the view is at least this wide"** — the largest `from` that fits wins,
wherever it sits in the list, and `from: 0` is the fallback. `flow` is `"row"` or
`"stack"`. **`share` divides width in either flow** — not row-only: a `"stack"` entry may
carry shares, and they set each band's width. It names the children by their **own child
names**, checked when the layout first arranges — a name with no child is a loud error.
**A share of `0` drops that child while the plan is active** — out of the flow, its space
reclaimed — so "the actions disappear on small screens" is one number rather than a
`narrow ? … : …` branch, and they return by themselves when the view widens.

**A child a plan does not name keeps its natural width, and a row's shares divide what
is left after it** — the gaps and the naturals both come out before the percentages are
cut, so `60` means 60% of the remainder and a fixed-width logo composes beside two
proportional regions with no arithmetic by hand. `"auto"` is the word for that said out
loud (`share: { logo: "auto", nav: 60, actions: 40 }`): identical geometry to leaving the
child out, and the opposite of `0` — `0` drops the child, `"auto"` keeps it at the size
it chose for itself. In a stack `"auto"` is accepted and changes nothing, since a stack's
shares are widths and its flow axis is height.

**Two values are refused where the plan is read.** A row whose shares add to more than
100 is an error naming the plan and the total — a row's shares divide one run and cannot
claim more of it than there is. (A stack's do not compete: each is an independent percent
of the view, so `{ logo: 100, nav: 100 }` is the full-bleed idiom, not an overflow — but a
single share above 100 is refused in either flow.) And a share that is neither a number
from 0 to 100 nor `"auto"` is an error naming the child, instead of a `NaN` width.

## gap
The default space between children, overridable per plan entry.

## place()
Selects the active plan from the **view's own width — never the window's** — and
arranges accordingly. That is what makes nesting work: the parent's plan decides how much
room a child gets, and the child's own plan answers to the room it got, automatically. A
plan can only name direct children; managing a grandchild is the child's plan's business.
*/

class ResponsiveLayout extends Layout [
    plan: array = { [] },
    gap: number = 10,

    place() {
        const view = this.view
        // the room the plan divides — the view's content box, its width less
        // its own padding (contentExtent); a plan's `from` therefore reads the room,
        // which is the number its shares are cut from
        const W = this.contentExtent("width")
        const kids = this.laid()
        const plans = (Array.isArray(this.plan) ? this.plan : [])

        // The active plan: the largest `from` that fits, wherever it sits in
        // the list; nothing fits → the smallest `from` (the nearest regime).
        let tier = null
        for (const t of plans) { const f = t.from ?? 0; if (W >= f && (tier == null || f > (tier.from ?? 0))) tier = t }
        if (tier == null) for (const t of plans) { if (tier == null || (t.from ?? 0) < (tier.from ?? 0)) tier = t }
        if (tier == null) tier = ({ flow: "row" })

        const stack = tier.flow == "stack"
        const gap = typeof tier.gap == "number" ? tier.gap : this.gap
        const share = tier.share != null && typeof tier.share == "object" ? tier.share : ({})

        // Resolve the share names against the view's own children — loudly —
        // and admit exactly two kinds of value: a percentage, and the word
        // "auto" ("keep this child at its natural size, divide the rest"),
        // which is carried by the plan and then means precisely what leaving
        // the child out means. Anything else is a mistake and is refused
        // here, because unrefused it would reach the arithmetic and come out
        // the other side as a NaN width on that child and a NaN x on its
        // neighbours, silently.
        const shares = new Map()
        const where = " (the plan from " + (tier.from ?? 0) + ", on " + view.constructor.name + ")"
        let total = 0
        for (const name of Object.keys(share)) {
            const c = view[name]
            if (c == null || kids.indexOf(c) < 0) {
                throw new Error("the plan names '" + name + "', but " + view.constructor.name + " has no laid child named '" + name + "'")
            }
            const v = share[name]
            if (v === "auto") continue
            if (typeof v != "number" || !isFinite(v) || v < 0 || v > 100) {
                throw new Error("the share for '" + name + "' is " + JSON.stringify(v) + where + " — a share is a number from 0 to 100 (and 0 drops the child), or the word \"auto\" to keep the child at its natural size")
            }
            shares.set(c, v)
            total = total + v
        }
        // A ROW's shares divide one run, so together they cannot claim more
        // than it — the sum is checked. A STACK's do not compete: each is an
        // independent band width, a plain percent of the view, and `100, 100`
        // (both bands full-bleed) is the documented idiom. Only the row is a
        // budget, so only the row has a total.
        if (!stack && total > 100 + 1e-6) {
            throw new Error("the row's shares add up to " + (Math.round(total * 100) / 100) + "%" + where + " — the shares of a row divide one run and cannot claim more than 100% of it; a child that should keep its natural size is named \"auto\" or left out of the share")
        }

        const hidden = (c) => shares.get(c) === 0
        const flexing = (c) => (c).flexes === true && !hidden(c)

        // What the shares divide, in a row: the width left after the gaps
        // between the visible children AND after the children the plan does
        // not allocate — one left out of the share, or named "auto", keeps
        // its natural width, and the shares are cut from what remains. That
        // is what makes "20%" mean 20% of what is left, so a fixed-width logo
        // composes beside two proportional regions with no arithmetic by
        // hand. (In a stack the flow axis is height, which a share never
        // touches, and a share is simply a percent of the view's width — so
        // naturals never compete for it and the base is the whole width.)
        let visN = 0
        for (const c of kids) if (c.visible && !hidden(c)) visN = visN + 1
        const avail = W - gap * Math.max(0, visN - 1)
        let naturalRun = 0
        if (!stack) {
            for (const c of kids) {
                if (!c.visible || hidden(c) || flexing(c) || shares.has(c)) continue
                naturalRun = naturalRun + (c).footprint().width
            }
        }
        const base = Math.max(0, avail - naturalRun)
        const shareW = (c) => (stack ? W : base) * (shares.get(c)) / 100

        // Rigid content along the flow axis, to size the spacers: widths in a
        // row (a share is a width), the children's own heights in a stack.
        let rigid = 0
        let flexN = 0
        for (const c of kids) {
            if (!c.visible || hidden(c)) continue
            if (flexing(c)) { flexN = flexN + 1; continue }
            // natural sizes are the footprint (scale/rotation composed,
            // position-free); a share or flex allocation stays the raw slot it
            // claims — the strategy never reads a slot it writes
            rigid = rigid + (stack ? (c).footprint().height : (shares.has(c) ? shareW(c) : (c).footprint().width))
        }
        const room = stack ? this.contentExtent("height") - gap * Math.max(0, visN - 1) : avail
        const each = flexN > 0 ? Math.max(0, room - rigid) / flexN : 0

        let pos = 0
        return kids.map((c) => {
            if (hidden(c)) {
                // excluded from the flow entirely: hidden, space reclaimed
                return stack ? ({ y: pos, vis: false }) : ({ x: pos, vis: false })
            }
            // allocated children (share, flex) take their slot raw; natural
            // ones are placed by footprint, lead-compensated
            const natural = !flexing(c) && !(stack ? false : shares.has(c))
            const f = natural ? (c).footprint() : null
            const lead = f == null ? 0 : (stack ? f.y : f.x)
            const box = (stack ? ({ y: pos - lead }) : ({ x: pos - lead }))
            if (shares.has(c)) box.w = shareW(c)                       // a share allocates width, both flows
            if (flexing(c)) box[stack ? "h" : "w"] = each              // a spacer flexes along the flow axis
            const adv = flexing(c) ? each
                      : stack      ? (f).height
                      : shares.has(c) ? shareW(c) : (f).width
            if (c.visible) pos = pos + adv + gap
            return box
            })
        }
    ]

/*
# Button

A labeled action control. The action is its `onClick` — write it at the use site
exactly as on any View; `Button` adds the interaction states, the themed look, and
keyboard activation (Space/Enter drives the same `onClick`). `primary = true` fills
with the accent; the default is the quiet control look.

```declare-fragment
Button [ label = "Save", primary = true ]
Button [ label = "Add", iconLeft = "PlusIcon" ]
Button [ label = "Options", menu = { app.optionsMenu } ]      // chevron implied
Button [ iconLeft = "CloseIcon" ]                             // icon only
```

Three slots in a row — `iconLeft`, the label, `iconRight` — laid out by
`SimpleLayout` and sized from `contentWidth`, so an absent label or icon costs
nothing and needs no special case: an invisible child is skipped and its space
reclaimed. A `menu` supplies the right icon on its own (`control.declare`).

The fill walks the four-rung ladder — `control` at rest, `controlHover`,
`controlPressed`, and `controlSelected` while its menu is open — so hovering a
button never looks like pressing it.

## label
The button's text. `""` (the default) leaves it iconless-and-wordless, which is
only useful with an `icon` — a button with neither has nothing to press.

## primary
Marks this as the affirmative action: it fills with the accent and takes
`theme.accentText` for its ink. One per group of choices — a dialog's Save, not
its Cancel. Purely a style decision; it changes nothing about behaviour.

```declare-fragment
Button [ label = "Cancel" ],
Button [ label = "Save", primary = true ]
```

## gap
Space between the icon and the label, when both are present.

## padH
Horizontal padding inside the button, each side. The button sizes to its content
plus twice this, so widening it is how you make a short label less cramped.

```declare-fragment
Button [ label = "OK", padH = 28 ]                  // a short word, given room
Button [ label = "Add", iconLeft = "PlusIcon", gap = 12 ]
```

## inkColor
The one ink decision, so a button's icon and its label always agree: the accent
text colour when `primary`, the ordinary text colour otherwise. `textColor`
follows it. Override this rather than `textColor` alone, or the icon and the
words drift apart.

```declare-fragment
class DangerButton extends Button [ inkColor = { primary ? white : crimson } ]
```

## baseline
The baseline a row aligns this control by — its label's, in the control's own
coordinates. A `SimpleLayout` or `WrappingLayout` with `align = baseline` sits the
button's text on the same line as a neighbouring `Text` or `Markdown`; without this
declaration the layout would refuse the button by name (a baseline is claimed, never
guessed from a box). Declared here so every button carries it; an icon-only button still
answers its (invisible) label's line.

```declare-fragment
row: View [ layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 10, align = baseline ],
    Text [ fontSize = 18, text = "Ready?" ],
    Button [ label = "Save", primary = true ]      // its label sits on the text's line
    ]
```

## height
The theme decides, when it has an opinion: `theme.buttonHeight` sets it (a platform
theme's native control height); a theme that leaves it unset gets 40. A `height` at
the use site overrides both.

## cornerRadius
A theme that rounds buttons by a fixed radius sets `theme.buttonRadius`; it is capped
at half the height, so a short button goes fully pill without a squared corner.
Otherwise the shared `controlRadius` every control uses.

## width
Content-sized: the run — icon, label, icon — plus `padH` each side. Give it a `width`
and the run centres inside it, so a dialog's equal-width row keeps every label centred.

## opacity
Disabled dims the whole control, icon and label alike, rather than recolouring it — to
the theme's `disabledOpacity`, or 0.45 when the theme states none.

## fill
The state ladder, one rung per interaction fact, first match wins: a primary button
is the accent whatever its state; an open menu holds the selected tint, so the
button stays lit while its menu is up; pressed beats hovered; at rest, the quiet
`control`. Every rung is a theme token, so a theme restyles every button's states
at once.

## stroke
A hairline only where the theme wants one: themes that border their secondary
buttons set `theme.buttonBorder`; flat themes leave it null and get no stroke. A
primary button is never bordered — the accent fill is its edge.

## press()
The activation path, called by a click and by Space/Enter. It flashes the press
tint, opens an attached `menu` if there is one, then calls `onClick`. Override
`onClick` for ordinary use; override this only to change what activation *means*.

## onClick
Your handler. Fires after the press is delivered, so the affordance has already
run — put the work here, not in `press()`.

## textColor
Follows `inkColor`, the button's one ink decision, so the label and the icon can never disagree. Override `inkColor`, not this.
*/



class Button extends Control [
    // the baseline a row aligns this control by: its label's (declared, never discovered)

    baseline: number = { this.run.y + this.run.cap.y + this.run.cap.baseline },
    height = { typeof this.theme.buttonHeight == "number" ? this.theme.buttonHeight : 40 },
    cornerRadius = { typeof this.theme.buttonRadius == "number" ? Math.min(this.theme.buttonRadius, this.height / 2) : this.theme.controlRadius },
    label:   string = "",
    primary: boolean = false,

    iconSize = 18,
    gap:  number = 8,
    padH: number = 16,

    onClick() { },
    press()   { this.flashPress(); $base.press(); this.onClick() },

    // one ink decision, so a primary button's icon and its label agree

    inkColor: Color = { this.primary ? this.theme.accentText : this.theme.text },
    textColor = { this.inkColor },

    // The run sizes to its own content and centers itself in the button; the
    // button's default width is the run plus symmetric padding. Two objects, so
    // the two facts stay independent: a button given an explicit `width` (a
    // dialog's equal-width row, a toolbar's fixed slot) still centers what it
    // holds, which a packed layout on the button itself would not.
    //
    // Not flexing Spacers: SimpleLayout reads the view's own extent to divide
    // slack, and the extent here is derived from the content — that closes a
    // cycle. A self-sizing child breaks it, because the run's width never looks
    // outward.

    width   = { this.run.width + this.padH * 2 },
    opacity = { this.disabled ? (typeof this.theme.disabledOpacity == "number" ? this.theme.disabledOpacity : 0.45) : 1 },

    fill = { this.primary   ? this.theme.accent
           : this.menuOpen  ? this.theme.controlSelected
           : this.down      ? this.theme.controlPressed
           : this.hot       ? this.theme.controlHover
           :             this.theme.control },

    // platforms whose secondary buttons are bordered say so in the theme
    // (Cupertino's and Redmond's hairline) — flat systems leave it null

    stroke  = { !this.primary && this.theme.buttonBorder != null ? stroke(1, this.theme.buttonBorder) : null },

    run: View [ height = 100%, x = center,
        width = { this.contentWidth },
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = { classroot.gap } ],

        ico:   IconHost [ kind = { classroot.iconLeft },  ink = { classroot.inkColor } ],
        cap:   TextLabel [ wrap = false, fontWeight = semibold,
            visible = { classroot.label != "" }, text = { classroot.label } ],
        trail: IconHost [ kind = { classroot.rightIcon }, ink = { classroot.inkColor } ]
        ]
    ]

/*
# FocusRing

The travelling focus indicator: a **shape-hugging** rounded outline that flies to
the focused control and reshapes to its own silhouette on arrival. The ring springs
position, size **and** `cornerRadius` together, which is why a Radio's row ring, a
pill Switch's ring and a text field's ring each differ — the targets do.

The model:

- **The fact** — the ring listens to Focus (a `Focus [ onFocusChange(v: View) { … } ]` member) and
  tracks the target's root-space geometry on every change, pointer or
  keyboard. A pointer focus **parks** the ring there silently (snap, no show) —
  so the next Tab's flight departs from the control the user just clicked,
  never from a stale perch.
- **Roles, theme-elected** — `theme.focusRing`: `"both"` (or `true`, the
  default) · `"travel"` (flight, then an idle fade) · `"rest"` (no flight —
  the ring appears in place and stays) · `false` (no ring; the theme's
  components carry the at-rest indication — the invariant lives with the
  theme-and-components pair). While keyboard modality holds and the role
  includes rest, the ring rests — no idle fade; a pointer press clears
  modality and the ring hides (web :focus-visible semantics).
- **The flight is the flourish** — `theme.focusAnimate = false` snaps every
  move; the underdamped travel is default-on.

It arrives automatically with any Control-descended component; declare your
own `FocusRing [ ]` (or subclass) to replace it. Pure chrome: never focusable,
never a pointer target. The silhouette is the target's `focusShape()` when it
declares one, else its bounds at its `cornerRadius`.

**You do not instantiate this, and you do not position it.** Tab between the controls
below and watch it fly; the members exist for a subclass that replaces the rendition,
and for the theme tokens that elect its behaviour.

```declare
row: View [ x = 20, y = 20,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 12 ],
    Button [ label = "One" ],
    Button [ label = "Two" ],
    Checkbox [ label = "Three" ]
    ]
```

## shown
Whether the ring is visible. **A pointer focus parks it silently** — geometry current,
ring hidden — so the next Tab departs from the control the user just clicked rather
than from a stale perch.

## placed
Whether the ring has ever had a target to park on. **The first appearance never flies**,
because a flight from nowhere is a streak across the app.

## ringRole()
The theme's election, from `theme.focusRing`: `"both"` (the default), `"travel"`
(flight, then fading once it settles), `"rest"` (appears in place and stays, no
flight), or `"off"`. **`"off"` is legitimate only when the theme's own controls carry
an at-rest focus indication** — the invariant belongs to the theme-and-components pair,
not to the ring alone.

## restRole()
Whether the elected role includes resting — a resting ring does not idle-fade.

## travelRole()
Whether the elected role includes the flight.

## snapTo()
Lands the ring on its target immediately, skipping the flight. What a pointer focus, a
first appearance, and `theme.focusAnimate = false` all use — **and what a target moving
under a resting ring uses**, so a dragged slider thumb carries the ring rigidly instead
of springing after it.

## overlayUp
True while an overlay owns the keyboard (an open `Menu`). **The ring stands down** —
the menu's own rover is the focus story, and a page ring poking around a panel reads as
chrome fighting chrome.

## idle
True once a travel-role ring has sat still long enough to fade out. A resting ring
never sets it.

## draw()
Renders the outline: four corner arcs with arms whose length is a pure function of
distance from the target, so the physics does the choreography. Override to replace the
rendition entirely. `theme.focusSights = true` restores the open-bracket "target
sights" reading; `theme.focusRingInnerWidth` adds the Fluent two-tone band.

## tx
**Internal.** The target x the springs chase.

## ty
**Internal.** The target y.

## tw
**Internal.** The target width.

## th
**Internal.** The target height.

## trad
**Internal.** The target corner radius — the silhouette reshapes as the ring travels.

## rad
**Internal.** The ring's current corner radius, sprung toward `trad`.

## gen
**Internal.** The idle-fade generation counter, so a retarget cancels the pending fade.

## visible
Visible while its faded opacity still shows anything, and never while an overlay is up — a ring behind a dialog would point at something you cannot reach.

## selectable
Chrome, never prose: the ring never selects inside a `selectable` region — the same provision every `Control` makes for its label.

## theme
The provided design-token record this widget styles off — a **provided value**
(the app provides it once with `App [ theme = { … } ]`; `Control` and the library
widgets read it). Reads `theme.accent` and its kin as typed members; defaults to the
house look. See `Control.theme`.
*/

class FocusRing extends View [
    // The design-token record, a provided value: the app provides it once and
    // this widget reads it, so `theme.x` reads below are plain typed members.
    // Default = the house look.

    theme: Theme = { this.$provided("theme", SanFrancisco) },
    opacity = 0,

    // an overlay owns the keys (an open menu — Keys.navClaim): the ring
    // stands down while it's up — the menu's rover is the focus story, and
    // a page ring poking around a panel reads as chrome fighting chrome

    overlayUp: boolean = false,
    claims: Keys [ onNavClaim(on: boolean) { classroot.overlayUp = on }
        ],

    // where the ring is headed — written by the subscription; the springs chase

    tx: number = 0,
    ty: number = 0,
    tw: number = 0,
    th: number = 0,
    trad: number = 8,
    rad: number = 8,
    shown: boolean = false,
    placed: boolean = false,                     // has the ring ever had a target to park on?

    // the idle fade — travel role only; a resting ring does not fade

    idle: boolean = false,
    gen:  number = 0,

    // role helpers over the theme election

    ringRole()  { const r = this.theme.focusRing; return r == false ? "off" : r == true || r == null ? "both" : r },
    restRole()  { const r = this.ringRole(); return r == "both" || r == "rest" },
    travelRole() { const r = this.ringRole(); return r == "both" || r == "travel" },

    snapTo() { this.x = this.tx; this.y = this.ty; this.width = this.tw; this.height = this.th; this.rad = this.trad },

    // the target's live silhouette, root-space, from the service's follower —
    // tracked through the parent chain and focusShape(), so an arrow-keyed
    // slider thumb (or a reflowing layout) moves the resting ring with it.
    // The band sits outside the silhouette: inner edge touching the control's
    // boundary (the Mac halo's hug), displaced by gap + half the stroke.

    focus: Focus [
        onGeometry(g: FocusGeometry) {
            if (g.root != this.root) return
            // Travel with the target's scroller: the ring's surface re-hosts
            // inside the scrolled container and positions in its content
            // coordinates — the platform then carries ring and control
            // together, zero lag, no per-scroll re-derive. A renderer without
            // the ride keeps root-space reactive positioning.
            const prev = (this)._lastTarget
            const prevHome = (this)._lastHome
            const hadHome = (this)._hasHome === true
            const px = classroot.x
            const py = classroot.y
            const homed = classroot.travelWith(g.scroller)
            const gx = homed ? g.homeX : g.x
            const gy = homed ? g.homeY : g.y
            // Crossing scroll homes (the bar's button → a control in the
            // scrolled page): the flight must still fly. The ring's current
            // position converts through root space into the destination
            // home's coordinates — same painted spot, new numbers — and the
            // springs then travel entirely within the new space (correct
            // even if that pane scrolls mid-flight).
            if (hadHome && prevHome != g.scroller) {
                const toRoot = prevHome == null || prevHome == this.root
                    ? ({ x: px, y: py })
                    : (() => { const o = prevHome.rootOrigin(); return ({ x: o.x + px - prevHome.scrollX, y: o.y + py - prevHome.scrollY }) })()
                const dst = !homed || g.scroller == this.root
                    ? toRoot
                    : (() => { const o = g.scroller.rootOrigin(); return ({ x: toRoot.x - o.x + g.scroller.scrollX, y: toRoot.y - o.y + g.scroller.scrollY }) })()
                classroot.x = dst.x
                classroot.y = dst.y
                }
            const gap = typeof classroot.theme.focusRingGap == "number" ? classroot.theme.focusRingGap : 0
            const lw = typeof classroot.theme.focusRingWidth == "number" ? classroot.theme.focusRingWidth : 3
            const iw = typeof classroot.theme.focusRingInnerWidth == "number" ? classroot.theme.focusRingInnerWidth : 0
            const off = gap + iw + lw / 2
            // a traveled ring draws the content-space box (homeW/homeH/homeRad):
            // any ancestor transform above the scroller reaches its surface
            // through the platform, so frame-space extents would double-apply it
            classroot.tx = gx - off; classroot.ty = gy - off
            classroot.tw = (homed ? g.homeW : g.w) + 2 * off; classroot.th = (homed ? g.homeH : g.h) + 2 * off
            classroot.trad = (homed ? g.homeRad : g.rad) + off
            // Only a focus change flies. The same control moving under a resting
            // ring — a dragged slider thumb, a reflow — takes the ring with it
            // rigidly: the springs' targets just moved, so land them now. The
            // discriminator is target identity (a handler-scratch expando — not
            // reactive state, just the previous event's view for comparison).
            ;(this)._lastTarget = g.view
            ;(this)._lastHome = g.scroller
            ;(this)._hasHome = true
            if (prev == g.view) classroot.snapTo()
            },

        onFocusChange(v: View) {
            if (v == null) { classroot.shown = false; return }
            let n = v
            for (; n != null && n.parent != null; n = n.parent) { }
            // Another app on the page (an embedded preview) owns this target — its own
            // ring indicates it; this one stands down rather than chase foreign coordinates.
            if (n != this.root) { classroot.shown = false; return }
            // Pointer focus: park silently — geometry current, ring hidden. The next
            // keyboard flight departs from here (the just-clicked control).
            if (!Focus.byKeyboard()) { classroot.shown = false; classroot.snapTo(); classroot.placed = true; return }
            if (classroot.ringRole() == "off") { classroot.shown = false; return }
            // No flight when: the ring has never been placed, the theme suppressed
            // the flourish, or the role is rest-only (appear in place, stay).
            if (!classroot.placed || classroot.theme.focusAnimate == false || !classroot.travelRole()) classroot.snapTo()
            classroot.placed = true
            classroot.shown = true
            classroot.raise()     // chrome stays chrome: above whatever raised last (a Dialog, a Menu)
            // idle fade only for the travel-only role — a resting ring persists
            if (classroot.restRole()) { classroot.idle = false; return }
            classroot.gen = classroot.gen + 1
            const g2 = classroot.gen
            classroot.idle = false
            setTimeout(() => { if (classroot.gen == g2) classroot.idle = true }, 1000)
            }
        ],

    // the travel: underdamped springs = motion toward the target + a little
    // arrival overshoot, by physics. The radius travels too: the terminus
    // reshapes to the control it surrounds.

    Spring [ attribute = x,      to = { classroot.tx }, stiffness = 220, damping = 16 ],
    Spring [ attribute = y,      to = { classroot.ty }, stiffness = 220, damping = 16 ],
    Spring [ attribute = width,  to = { classroot.tw }, stiffness = 220, damping = 18 ],
    Spring [ attribute = height, to = { classroot.th }, stiffness = 220, damping = 18 ],
    Spring [ attribute = rad,    to = { classroot.trad }, stiffness = 220, damping = 20 ],

    Spring [ attribute = opacity, to = { classroot.shown && !classroot.idle ? 0.9 : 0 }, stiffness = 90, damping = 20 ],

    visible = { this.opacity > 0.02 && !this.overlayUp },

    // pure chrome: never a pointer target — and never on the hover chain
    // either, which is what pointerEvents (unlike a hit-test override) governs

    pointerEvents = "none",

    // chrome, not prose: never selects inside a `selectable` region

    selectable = false,

    // the shape-hugging outline — a rounded rect at the sprung radius, drawn,
    // so it re-derives as the radius travels between silhouettes

    // the corner brackets and the shape-hugging outline are one rendition at
    // two coverages: four corner-anchored strokes whose straight arms extend
    // toward each edge's midpoint by cov ∈ (0..1]. cov = 1 → arms meet, a
    // closed outline; cov low → open corner brackets ("target sights").
    // Coverage is a pure function of distance from target, so the physics
    // does the choreography: a flight breaks the ring open mid-air and the
    // settle closes the sights around the new silhouette. Small targets skip
    // the bracket phase (a ring around a radio dot has no arms to open).

    draw(d: Draw) {
        const w = this.width, h = this.height
        const Q = Math.PI / 2
        const dist = Math.abs(this.x - this.tx) + Math.abs(this.y - this.ty) + Math.abs(this.width - this.tw) + Math.abs(this.height - this.th)
        const small = Math.min(w, h) < 40
        // By default the ring travels as a closed outline, source shape to
        // destination shape — no bracket mode intervening. `theme.focusSights
        // = true` elects the sights rendition instead (data, not a code path):
        // fully open in motion, arms sweeping shut inside the last 16px of
        // settle.
        const cov = this.theme.focusSights == true && !small ? Math.max(0.22, 1 - dist / 16) : 1
        const lw = typeof this.theme.focusRingWidth == "number" ? this.theme.focusRingWidth : 3
        // The indicator is one band or two, by data: `focusRingInnerWidth` > 0
        // adds an inner stroke between the control and the outer one — the
        // Redmond two-tone focus rectangle, black over white. Both passes are
        // the same outline at different insets; the flight, the sights, and
        // the silhouette machinery are shared, not forked.
        const iw = typeof this.theme.focusRingInnerWidth == "number" ? this.theme.focusRingInnerWidth : 0
        const pass = (ins, lwp, color) => {
            const r = Math.max(1, Math.min(this.rad - ins, Math.min(w, h) / 2 - ins))
            const ax = Math.max(0, ((w - 2 * ins) / 2 - r) * cov)      // horizontal arm length
            const ay = Math.max(0, ((h - 2 * ins) / 2 - r) * cov)      // vertical arm length
            d.strokeStyle = color
            d.lineWidth = lwp
            d.beginPath()
            // each corner: arc + arms, inset symmetrically
            d.moveTo(ins + r + ax, ins); d.lineTo(ins + r, ins); d.arc(ins + r, ins + r, r, -Q, Math.PI, true); d.lineTo(ins, ins + r + ay)
            d.moveTo(w - ins - r - ax, ins); d.lineTo(w - ins - r, ins); d.arc(w - ins - r, ins + r, r, -Q, 0); d.lineTo(w - ins, ins + r + ay)
            d.moveTo(w - ins, h - ins - r - ay); d.lineTo(w - ins, h - ins - r); d.arc(w - ins - r, h - ins - r, r, 0, Q); d.lineTo(w - ins - r - ax, h - ins)
            d.moveTo(ins + r + ax, h - ins); d.lineTo(ins + r, h - ins); d.arc(ins + r, h - ins - r, r, Q, Math.PI); d.lineTo(ins, h - ins - r - ay)
            d.stroke()
            }
        pass(0, lw, typeof this.theme.focusColor == "number" ? this.theme.focusColor : this.theme.accent)
        if (iw > 0) pass((lw + iw) / 2, iw, typeof this.theme.focusRingInnerColor == "number" ? this.theme.focusRingInnerColor : this.theme.surface)
        }
    ]

/*
# Tooltip

The layer system's floor: any view sets `tip = "…"` and this singleton renders
it — hover delay, placement, flip, and theming are nobody's problem at the use
site. It arrives automatically when any view in the program carries a tip, as
`FocusRing` does; declare your own `Tooltip [ ]` (or subclass) to replace the
rendition.

It listens to the Tip service (a `Tip [ onTip(e: TipEvent) { … } ]` member), which owns the platform
conventions (the show delay — `tooltipDelay`, ~1s in the Cupertino and Redmond
conventions, ~500ms MountainView — instant retarget while showing, hide on
press). **Placement is theme data too** (`tooltipPlacement`): "below" (the
default — under the target, centered, flipping above at the edge), "above"
(flipping below), or "pointer" (the Cupertino help tag —
offset below-right of where the cursor rests). Always clamped inside the
app. Pure chrome — never focusable, never a pointer target, always above
content (the auto-splice puts it last in the App, and source order stacks).

Theming (the four cities carry the tokens): `tooltipBg` / `tooltipText` /
`tooltipLine` (null = no border) / `tooltipSize` (11 in Cupertino, 12
elsewhere). Cupertino and Redmond wear bordered surface chips; San
Francisco and Mountain View the inverse chip.

**You do not instantiate this.** Set `tip` on any view and the singleton appears; the
members below exist for a subclass that replaces the rendition.

```declare
View [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 130, height = 38, cornerRadius = 8, fill = steelblue,
    tip = "Saves your changes",
    Text [ x = center, y = center, textColor = white, text = "Save" ]
    ]
```

## tipText
The text currently displayed, pushed in by the `Tip` service from the hovered view's
`tip`. **A subclass reads this; nothing writes it from app code.**

## shown
Whether a tip is currently up. Driven by the service's delay, retarget and hide rules —
not by you.

## placement
Where the chip sits, elected by `theme.tooltipPlacement`: `"below"` (the default,
centred under the target, flipping above at the edge), `"above"` (the Fluent default,
flipping below), or `"pointer"` (the Cupertino help tag, offset below-right of the resting
cursor). Always clamped inside the app.

## ex
**Internal.** The target's root-space x, for placement.

## ey
**Internal.** The target's root-space y.

## ew
**Internal.** The target's width.

## eh
**Internal.** The target's height.

## px
**Internal.** The pointer's x where it came to rest, for `"pointer"` placement.

## py
**Internal.** The pointer's y where it came to rest.

## visible
Shown only when asked and there is text to show — an empty tip never paints a bare box.

## width
Sized to its text plus padding, so the box fits the words and never the other way round.

## height
Sized to its text plus padding, one line or several.

## x
Beside the pointer, or centred under the element, then clamped inside the app with a 4px margin — a tip near an edge slides in rather than clipping.

## y
Below the pointer, or below or above the element as `placement` says, flipping to the other side when the preferred one would leave the app.

## fill
The theme's tooltip ground when it names one, else a dark slate — a tip is the one surface that stays dark on a light theme.

## stroke
A hairline only when the theme names a tooltip line; flat themes get none.

## selectable
Chrome, never prose: a tip's text does not select, even inside a `selectable` region — the same provision every `Control` makes for its label.

## theme
The provided design-token record this widget styles off — a **provided value**
(the app provides it once with `App [ theme = { … } ]`; `Control` and the library
widgets read it). Reads `theme.accent` and its kin as typed members; defaults to the
house look. See `Control.theme`.
*/

class Tooltip extends View [
    // The design-token record, a provided value: the app provides it once and
    // this widget reads it, so `theme.x` reads below are plain typed members.
    // Default = the house look.

    theme: Theme = { this.$provided("theme", SanFrancisco) },
    visible = { this.shown && this.tipText != "" },

    tipText: string = "",
    shown:   boolean = false,
    ex: number = 0,                              // the target's root-space box
    ey: number = 0,
    ew: number = 0,
    eh: number = 0,
    px: number = 0,                              // the pointer at show (the "pointer" placement)
    py: number = 0,

    tips: Tip [
        onTip(e: TipEvent) {
            if (e == null || e.root != this.root) { classroot.shown = false; return }
            classroot.ex = e.x; classroot.ey = e.y; classroot.ew = e.w; classroot.eh = e.h
            classroot.px = this.root.pointerX; classroot.py = this.root.pointerY   // the pointer as it rests at show
            classroot.tipText = e.text
            classroot.shown = true
            classroot.raise()     // chrome stays chrome: above whatever raised last
            }
        ],

    // geometry derives from the label's measured size; placement is a theme
    // election (below | above | pointer), always flipped when it would leave
    // the app and clamped horizontally

    placement: string = { typeof this.theme.tooltipPlacement == "string" ? this.theme.tooltipPlacement : "below" },

    width  = { this.t.width + 16 },
    height = { this.t.height + 10 },
    x = { Math.max(4, Math.min(this.root.width - this.width - 4,
            this.placement == "pointer" ? this.px + 10 : this.ex + (this.ew - this.width) / 2)) },
    y = { this.placement == "pointer"
            ? (this.py + 18 + this.height > this.root.height ? this.py - this.height - 8 : this.py + 18)
            : this.placement == "above"
                ? (this.ey - this.height - 7 < 0 ? this.ey + this.eh + 7 : this.ey - this.height - 7)
                : (this.ey + this.eh + 7 + this.height > this.root.height ? this.ey - this.height - 7 : this.ey + this.eh + 7) },

    cornerRadius = 4,
    fill   = { typeof this.theme.tooltipBg == "number" ? this.theme.tooltipBg : 0x1B2733 },
    stroke = { typeof this.theme.tooltipLine == "number" ? stroke(1, this.theme.tooltipLine) : null },

    // pure chrome: never a pointer target — and never on the hover chain
    // either, which is what pointerEvents (unlike a hit-test override) governs.
    // "none" is a corridor, not a lid (View.pointerEvents), so the label must
    // carry it too, or it would answer viewAt and sit on the hover chain.

    pointerEvents = "none",

    // chrome, not prose: a tip never selects inside a `selectable` region

    selectable = false,

    t: Text [ pointerEvents = "none",
        x = 8, y = 5, fontSize = { typeof classroot.theme.tooltipSize == "number" ? classroot.theme.tooltipSize : 12 },
        textColor = { typeof classroot.theme.tooltipText == "number" ? classroot.theme.tooltipText : 0xF4F6FA },
        text = { classroot.tipText } ]
    ]

/*
# What's under the hood

An explanatory piece for readers who know front-end frameworks and have read
Declare's homepage, the "Why" essay and the FAQ. Its premise: a Declare front
end is one complete artifact, and the runtime is what has to be true underneath
for that to hold. It follows one program from source to pixels: what the
compiler produces, what a click does, who decides where things are, where the
host begins, and how a Declare program meets code that isn't Declare.

The page is itself a Declare program, and its figures are live views rather
than pictures: morphs are sprung scalars, and the hit-testing figure reports
the runtime's own answers. Open it with `?render=canvas` and it is the same
page, drawn directly to pixels.

Sizes quoted here were measured 2026-09-18 from real `declarec` production
builds (gzip, default level).
*/



// The page's palette, light and dark. A token record is the language's own
// notation for this, so the tokens are declarations rather than a JavaScript
// object a `{ }` body happens to hold — and the App spreads one over a library
// preset below, so the few library widgets on the page find every token they
// read. The five layer hues (app, lib, run, ker, host) are the page's code.

theme Paper [
    ground = #F4F6F7, panel = #FFFFFF, sunk = #EBEFF1, line = #D5DDE3,
    ink = #15202A, lead = #34434F, body = #2A3843, muted = #5B6B78, faint = #8796A2,
    codeBg = #E8EDF0,
    accent = #2F6BDE, accentHover = #1F55BD,
    app = #2F6BDE, appSoft = #E3ECFC,
    lib = #0F9488, libSoft = #DDF3EF,
    run = #CF7B22, runSoft = #FBEBD8, runSoft0 = #FDF5EB,
    ker = #C23A5C, kerSoft = #F8E1E7,
    host = #6B7788, hostLine = #9AA6B3, hostSoft = #EDF0F3
    ]


theme Ink [
    ground = #0E1418, panel = #151D23, sunk = #1A242B, line = #26333D,
    ink = #E6ECF0, lead = #BBC7D0, body = #C9D3DA, muted = #8E9DA9, faint = #66757F,
    codeBg = #1E2931,
    accent = #6E9CF5, accentHover = #93B6FA,
    app = #5F8FF0, appSoft = #17263F,
    lib = #2DB7A7, libSoft = #11302D,
    run = #E59A45, runSoft = #3A2A17, runSoft0 = #221C15,
    ker = #E3637F, kerSoft = #3A1C25,
    host = #8C98A8, hostLine = #5A6775, hostSoft = #1B242B
    ]


App [ appName = "What's under the hood · Declare",

    // hosted: when a page mounts this program in an AppIsland (the homepage's
    // Architecture view), the host provides its appearance and type (read
    // here with hostProvided), and the page lengthens instead of this app scrolling
    // itself — so the host's own page scroll carries the article. Standalone,
    // the defaults hold and the app follows the system.

    hosted: boolean = { this.$hostProvided("hosted", false) },
    face: string = { this.$hostProvided("face", "") },
    headWeight: number = { this.$hostProvided("headWeight", 600) },
    inDark: boolean = { this.$hostProvided("dark", this.root.dark) },
    headTrack: number = { this.root.headWeight >= 900 ? -0.006 : this.root.headWeight >= 700 ? -0.014 : -0.02 },

    // this app's full height, exposed for the host to size its island to
    // (the host reads it as `island.exposed("docH", 0)`)

    exposes = ["docH"],
    docH: number = { this.page.y + this.page.height },

    scrolls = { this.root.hosted ? "none" : "y" },
    theme = { this.root.inDark ? ({ ...SanFranciscoDark, ...Ink }) : ({ ...SanFrancisco, ...Paper }) },

    // hosted, the app paints no ground: the host's own paper shows through

    fill = { this.root.hosted ? null : this.$provided("theme").ground },

    // Helvetica, as on the homepage — unless a host hands down another face

    uiFace: string[] = { this.root.face == "avenir" ? Faces.avenir : Faces.helvetica },
    fontFamily = { this.root.uiFace },
    fontSize = 17,
    textColor = { this.$provided("theme").body },

    // the two measures every edge on the page derives from

    col: number = { Math.min(680, this.root.width - 40) },
    wide: number = { Math.min(1060, this.root.width - 32) },

    // Fig. 1's two states: the reader switches between them

    heroMode: string = "looks",
    heroActual: boolean = { this.root.heroMode == "is" },

    // SELECTION IS PROVIDED ONCE, here, for the whole article. `selectable` is
    // a provided value, so a container opts its subtree in and each text leaf
    // reads the nearest provision — the type classes in the kit say nothing
    // about it, and a rich-text flow is selectable by its nature regardless.
    // The controls inside the figures are unaffected: every `Control` provides
    // `selectable = false` for its own label, which is the language's rule that
    // anything you activate is not copy.

    // The air above and below the article is the column's own padding. A padded
    // view has an inside: every child is placed from it, and the automatic
    // height counts the trailing inset back in, so `docH` measures the whole
    // article.

    page: View [ width = { this.root.col }, x = center, padding = [72, 0, 72, 0], selectable = true,
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 40 ],

        // ── the masthead ──────────────────────────────────────────────

        masthead: View [ width = 100%,
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 18 ],
            Eyebrow [ text = "Declare · runtime architecture" ],
            Title [ fontSize = { this.root.width < 700 ? 40 : 54 }, text = "What’s under the hood" ],
            Dek [ text = "A Declare front end is one self-contained artifact — small enough to hold in your head, strict enough for a machine to verify, rich enough for an ambitious application. This is what Declare’s runtime does to make that true." ]
            ],

        // ── the lede ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

        lede: View [ width = 100%,
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 22 ],
            Para [ html = "Declare asks you to believe something unusual about an application’s front end: that it’s complete. Everything the interface does is written in the one program — where each box goes, how a paragraph wraps, what a click landed on, how a view becomes the next one, how a list of a million rows scrolls on a phone. It can be written that way because the program states the <i>relationships</i> between its elements rather than the steps that maintain them — which is both easier to understand and substantially more compact. A machine can verify it whole. And because the compiler sees every relationship in advance, the runtime never has to discover anything while it runs — which is why it was possible to write its hot path once, in a few kilobytes of C." ],
            Para [ html = "A promise like that puts a demand on what runs underneath. If the browser decided the layout, the program wouldn’t be complete. If a click were routed by the DOM, the same interface drawn directly to pixels would route it differently. So the runtime makes those decisions itself, once — and then hands the browser everything it is genuinely best at: real text, native fields, the page’s own scroll, links that behave like links. Declare isn’t a native framework running in a browser tab; it’s a language for interfaces with the feel of good native software that are also good citizens of the web — crawlable, selectable, responsive, touch-native. That the same program also runs drawn directly to pixels, and in a Mac application with no browser in it, is the proof that it really is complete: nothing about it was ever decided somewhere else. Figure 1 is that division of labor, before and after." ]
            ],

        // ── Fig. 1 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

        fig1: Figure [
            Caption [ fig = "Fig. 1 — Two pictures of one program",
                text = "Left: the stack a web framework implies — your file, a framework, and a browser that decides layout, text, and what you touched. Right: what runs — the compiled program, the library (itself written in Declare), a runtime that owns every one of those decisions, a small C kernel beneath it, and a host on the far side of one interface that realizes the result. The same five colors mark the same layers throughout." ],
            top: View [
                Segmented [ x = center, width = 300, height = 32,
                    value = { this.root.heroActual ? "is" : "looks" },
                    choices = { [ ({ id: "looks", label: "What it looks like" }), ({ id: "is", label: "What it is" }) ] },
                    input(v: object) { this.root.heroMode = "" + v }
                    ]
                ],
            Plate [ pad = 0,
                stage: HeroStage [ k = 0,
                    Spring [ attribute = k, to = { this.root.heroActual ? 1 : 0 }, stiffness = 60, damping = 14 ]
                    ]
                ],
            keys: View [
                legend: View [ x = center,
                    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 16 ],
                    Key [ hue = { this.$provided("theme").app }, label = "App" ],
                    Key [ hue = { this.$provided("theme").lib }, label = "Library" ],
                    Key [ hue = { this.$provided("theme").run }, label = "Runtime" ],
                    Key [ hue = { this.$provided("theme").ker }, label = "Kernel" ],
                    Key [ hue = { this.$provided("theme").hostLine }, label = "Host" ]
                    ]
                ]
            ],

        // ── 01 · The compiler ────────────────────────────────────────

        compile: Section [ num = "01", head = "The compiler",
            Para [ html = "The compiler is where completeness is enforced. It parses the brackets into a tree, resolves every bare name to an explicit path, extracts the read set of every <code>{ }</code> body, and type-checks each one as TypeScript against the real interface of every component — so an attribute that doesn’t exist, or a relationship that reads something it can’t, is an error before anything runs. What it emits is not code that builds a UI. It is a description of one: the tree with its attributes, a graph of which value reads which, and — for every body that is plain arithmetic over numeric slots — bytecode for a small stack machine." ],
            Para [ html = "One line from the homepage’s bouncing-ball demo, as it appears in the compiled artifact:" ],
            Trace [ ],
            Para [ html = "That last line never runs as JavaScript. In the calendar about 600 bodies take this path; the rest compile to ordinary functions the runtime calls back when their inputs change. A framework learns what depends on what while it runs — by re-running a component, or from a list you keep by hand. Here it is read off the source once, and the runtime never has to discover anything." ],
            Para [ html = "Because the program is data, it can be questioned as data. The Inspector on any page here — ⌥⌘D — reads this same graph: click a value and it shows the body that produced it, every path that body read, and their live values. That is not a debugging layer added to the runtime. It is the compiler’s output, still present at run time, answering a query." ]
            ],

        // ── 02 · What a click does ───────────────────────────────────

        run: Section [ num = "02", head = "What a click does",
            Para [ html = "When a handler writes an attribute, nothing updates yet. The write is queued. When the handler returns, the runtime performs one update: it finds every value that read what changed, recomputes each exactly once, in dependency order, and only then tells the host what moved. Declare calls that update a <b>settle</b> — the graph, disturbed by a write, coming back to rest — and it is the whole of the update model. If you know React, it is the nearest thing to a commit, except that nothing was rendered to compare against: the graph already knows what depends on what. The whole trip takes about a millisecond." ],
            Figure [
                Caption [ fig = "Fig. 2 — What a click sets in motion",
                    text = "The meter is live; its button is a library Button. Raise the level, then step through what your click set in motion. Four lanes: host, runtime, your code, kernel. Steps 5 and 6 are the split the compiler arranged in advance — arithmetic runs in the kernel; a template string is not arithmetic, so the kernel calls back into your compiled TypeScript, inside the same settle." ],
                ClickFig [ ]
                ],
            Para [ html = "Steps 2 and 3 are the ones a web framework leaves to the browser: which element is under the pointer, and whether this was a click or the start of a drag. Here the runtime answers both, so hover, press, click-versus-drag and gesture ownership are one implementation — the same under a finger as under a pointer, and the same on every renderer." ],
            Para [ html = "Step 5 runs no JavaScript at all. Beneath the runtime sits a kernel: one C file, about 940 lines, that holds the graph’s tables — every attribute, every edge, the settle’s order — and evaluates the arithmetic the compiler turned into bytecode. In the browser it ships as WebAssembly, 11 KB gzipped inside the bundle; on the Mac the same source is linked in natively. It is unusual for a web framework to carry a C library, and it is only possible because the compiler left nothing for the runtime to work out: with every dependency and every arithmetic body known in advance, the bookkeeping of a settle is a fixed procedure over fixed tables, which is what C is for. A frame that only moves geometry — most animation frames — finishes inside it without entering JavaScript." ]
            ],

        // ── 03 · Motion ──────────────────────────────────────────────

        motion: Section [ num = "03", head = "Motion",
            Para [ html = "Continuity — a view becoming the next one rather than being replaced by it — is the part of good native software that is usually bespoke. Here is what makes it ordinary at run time. A <code>Spring</code> is an attribute with a target; each frame it writes its next value, and that write is a settle like any other — the same graph, the same kernel, the same dependency order. Nothing about animation is special-cased. Because geometry arithmetic runs as bytecode, a frame that only moves things — most frames of most animations — completes inside the kernel, in WebAssembly, and never enters JavaScript. Because a settle is one indivisible step, a new target arriving mid-flight is simply the next write: the spring retargets from wherever it is, and every value that read it follows. Interruptibility isn’t a feature of the animation system; it is what a reactive graph does when you write to it. And a layout is re-derived in the same settle, so an arrangement can travel as one thing — the calendar’s four views are one surface under a moving rectangle, and each frame of that zoom is a settle over the same few hundred kernel bodies." ]
            ],

        // ── 04 · Layout and hit-testing ──────────────────────────────

        geometry: Section [ num = "04", head = "Layout and hit-testing",
            Para [ html = "Open a Declare page in the browser’s element inspector and every view is there — a <code>div</code> per component, nested to mirror the tree — with its position and size written on it as inline style and no layout CSS anywhere. That is not a shortcut. It is the division of labor: every box is decided before the host hears about it, by one of three things. Arithmetic over slots (<code>x = { parent.width - 40 }</code>) runs in the kernel. A layout is a library class with one method, <code>place()</code>, returning a box per child — 1.8 KB of runtime applies it. An unset width is the bounding box of the visible children, kept current as a rule over the geometry table. Text is the one place the host contributes a number: it measures glyph widths. Wrapping, line count and the resulting height are the runtime’s, so a paragraph breaks into the same lines on every renderer." ],
            Para [ html = "Because the runtime placed every box, it can answer the inverse question — what is under this point? — and that answer decides hover, press, whether a gesture was a click, and where a drag lands. The scene below computes none of it. Each view is reporting its own <code>hovered</code> fact, kept true by the same walk that routes a press." ],
            Figure [
                Caption [ fig = "Fig. 3 — Ask the runtime what is under the pointer",
                    text = "Try the rotated card’s corners, the empty-looking glass, the badge, and the child where the pane’s clip cuts it off. The walk runs in reverse paint order and stops at the first view that has declared an interest in the pointer." ],
                HitFig [ ]
                ],
            Para [ html = "Drawn to pixels, or on the Mac, there is no DOM to ask, so this walk is the whole of hit-testing: clips subtracted, transforms inverted, scrollers corrected. In the DOM the browser names the element and the runtime resolves it to the nearest view with a handler. Either way the decision is one implementation, and the same one that decides whether a touch is a tap, a scroll, or a drag the page should not claim. Your program can ask it directly — <code>app.viewAt(x, y)</code> — which is how drag and drop is written." ]
            ],

        // ── 05 · Where the host begins ───────────────────────────────

        hosts: Section [ num = "05", head = "Where the host begins",
            Para [ html = "Everything above happens on one side of a single interface. It is called <code>Surface</code>, it has 58 methods — <code>setX</code>, <code>setFill</code>, <code>setText</code>, <code>setDrawing</code> — the runtime calls them and a backend implements them, and a view never learns which backend it has. Below that interface a backend does one job: realize what it was told. The DOM backend keeps a <code>div</code> per view with its geometry as inline style, and hands the platform what the platform does best — real text that selects and finds, the page’s own scroll, links that ⌘-click, native form fields. The canvas backend draws the whole tree directly to pixels, with its own compositor and its own scroll physics, for surfaces that must be uniform or capturable. The Mac host is a Swift application with no web engine in it: it runs the same runtime bundle in JavaScriptCore, links the same kernel as C, and applies one batch of drawing operations per settle to a tree of Core Animation layers." ],
            f4: Figure [
                Caption [ fig = "Fig. 4 — One program, three hosts",
                    text = "The calendar’s build, drawn to scale by gzipped bytes. Switch hosts and watch what moves: the program, the library and the rest of the runtime are the same bytes; one block is exchanged, and on the Mac the kernel leaves the bundle for the executable. Beneath, the same desktop program with the same four windows open, on each host." ],
                sf: ShipsFig [ ]
                ],
            Para [ html = "Drawing crosses the interface the same way. A view that needs a mark attributes can’t express — a gauge, a sparkline, an icon — defines <code>draw(d)</code>. It looks like canvas code but it records; the recording is plain data that the DOM backend rasterizes into the view’s own element, the canvas backend replays during its composite, and the Mac replays through Core Graphics. A drawing that read nothing that changed costs nothing." ],
            Para [ html = "Declare’s own model is single-threaded by construction: a settle is one step, and there is nothing in the language that runs concurrently with it. That is what lets hosts thread freely on their side of <code>Surface</code> — the canvas backend rasterizes drawings in a worker, and the Mac runs the runtime on a thread of its own and commits to the display on another. Your program never sees any of it." ]
            ],

        // ── 06 · Islands ─────────────────────────────────────────────

        islands: Section [ num = "06", head = "Interoperability",
            Para [ html = "<code>Surface</code> is the boundary between the runtime and its host. There is a second boundary, between a Declare program and code that isn’t Declare, and it has the same shape in every direction. A page embeds a Declare app by marking a sized element as its host — one attribute, <code>data-declare-embed</code> — and the app runs in that box like any other element on the page. The interface between them is declared, not improvised: the page <i>provides</i> values the app reads with <code>hostProvided</code>, the app <i>exposes</i> values the page reads back, and either side can <code>post</code> a message the other receives in <code>onPost</code>. Inside a Declare program the same contract runs the other way. A <code>DOMIsland</code> is a box the tree sizes and positions, inside which a video player, an editor or a React widget owns its own DOM. An <code>AppIsland</code> hosts a second Declare program as a tenant with the same provides, exposes and post — this article is one, mounted inside the homepage, taking its theme and type from the page around it. Three directions, one boundary, one vocabulary." ]
            ],

        // ── 07 · Why it still reads like a web page ──────────────────

        why: Section [ num = "07", head = "What remains the browser’s",
            Para [ html = "A Declare app in a browser is a web page, not an imitation of one. The behaviors that make a page a page stay the browser’s, and Declare does not simulate them: text is real text — it selects, it finds, it copies; fields are native fields, with native carets and input methods; scroll and overscroll are the page’s own; links are links, ⌘-click and all, and the Back button tells the truth; a crawler reads a static extraction from a plain host. The DOM renderer is the default because nothing about the program’s meaning is at stake in the browser, so all of that can be left to the browser without risk. A reader would struggle to tell a Declare page from any other." ],
            Para [ html = "What keeps that description honest is the second rendering. A program that owns its own semantics — no stylesheet, no browser layout engine to defer to — can be checked against another realization of itself, and the platform’s test suite holds the DOM renderer and the pixel renderer to the same picture on every change. The discipline that makes a front end complete is the same one that makes it mean exactly one thing." ],
            links: View [ width = 100%,
                layout: WrappingLayout [ spacing = 22, lineSpacing = 10 ],
                Text [ fontSize = 15, fontWeight = medium, link = "apps/architecture/architecture.declare?render=canvas",
                    textColor = { this.hovered ? this.$provided("theme").accentHover : this.$provided("theme").accent },
                    text = "Reopen this page on the canvas renderer →" ],
                Text [ fontSize = 15, fontWeight = medium, link = "apps/architecture/architecture.declare?viewer=reader",
                    textColor = { this.hovered ? this.$provided("theme").accentHover : this.$provided("theme").accent },
                    text = "Read this page’s source →" ]
                ]
            ],

        colophon: View [ width = 100%,
            layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 10 ],

            // nothing here claims a width — a y-axis stack never does — so the
            // rule derives its own from the parent's content box

            Divider [ ],
            Small [ width = 100%,
                text = "This page is a Declare program; every figure on it is made of views. Sizes were measured on 18 September 2026 from declarec production builds, gzipped at the default level." ]
            ]
        ]
    ,
    // the traveling focus indicator — provided with the component library

    FocusRing [ ],

    // the tooltip singleton — provided with the component library (tip = attribute)

    Tooltip [ ],
]

